- Racial or Ethnic Background: 74% Similar / 26% Different
- Level of Income: 56% Similar / 44% Different
- Level of Education: 63% Similar / 37% Different
- Social Status: 70% Similar / 30% Different
- Political Views: 62% Similar / 38% Different
- Life Stage: 69% Similar / 31% Different]', 'Based on the information above, it is possible to say that', '[{"letra": "A", "texto": "7,400 people affirmed not having friends mostly similar to their racial or ethnic background."}, {"letra": "B", "texto": "6,200 people declared simultaneously having mostly similar friends considering their religious beliefs and level of education."}, {"letra": "C", "texto": "the amount of people who answered that their friends are mostly similar to them when it comes to their level of income is 5,600."}, {"letra": "D", "texto": "the amount of people that affirmed having mostly similar friends considering their life stage is bigger than the ones who admit having mostly similar friends with the same social status."}]', 'C', 'De acordo com o gráfico, 56% dos 10.000 entrevistados responderam que seus amigos possuem nível de renda (Level of Income) similar ao seu. Calculando 56% de 10.000 obtém-se exatamente 5.600 pessoas.', 'Gráficos de pizza indicando as porcentagens de "Mostly Similar" vs "Mostly Different" para cada área.', '[{"titulo": "Passo 1", "conteudo": "De acordo com o gráfico, 56% dos 10.000 entrevistados responderam que seus amigos possuem nível de renda (Level of Income) similar ao seu.", "formula": null}, {"titulo": "Fazendo a conta", "conteudo": "Calculando 56% de 10.000 obtém-se exatamente 5.600 pessoas.", "formula": null}]', 'automatica', FALSE, FALSE, '09ea97e9fc830512fee48dec0640538584800994f314e7a79240d50c52a050ce', 'epcar-2024.txt'),
('EPCAR', 'Inglês', 'Comparatives and Superlatives', 2024, NULL, 'EPCAR / DENSINO', 48, '48', NULL, 'facil', '(Utilizar os TEXTOS III e IV)', 'How do texts III and IV relate to each other?', '[{"letra": "A", "texto": "Even being different, people can still be friends."}, {"letra": "B", "texto": "They say that opposite people are better friends."}, {"letra": "C", "texto": "People sharing the same ideas tend to become closer friends."}, {"letra": "D", "texto": "Friendship is stronger when you have friends with opposite tastes."}]', 'A', 'O texto III mostra que duas pessoas totalmente opostas (Enid e Wandinha) se tornam amigas, enquanto o texto IV aborda estatisticamente a diversidade entre amigos, demonstrando que pessoas diferentes ainda assim podem construir amizades sólidas.', NULL, NULL, 'resumida', FALSE, FALSE, '275834a51b3819cc200047e83978fd4949432d056003976c39b2987252d7f40d', 'epcar-2024.txt'),
('EPCAR', 'Inglês', 'Reading Comprehension', 2025, NULL, 'EPCAR / FAB', 1, '01', NULL, 'medio', 'TEXT I
"I showed the grown-ups my masterpiece, and I asked them if my drawing scared them. They answered, ''Why be scared of a hat?'' My drawing was not a picture of a hat. It was a picture of a boa constrictor digesting an elephant.
Then I drew the inside of the boa constrictor, so the grown-ups could understand. They always need explanations. My Drawing Number Two looked like this:
The grown-ups'' advised me to put away my drawings of boa constrictors, outside or inside, and apply myself instead to geography, history, arithmetic, and grammar. That is why, I abandoned, at the age of six, a magnificent career as an artist. I had been discouraged by the failure of my Drawing Number One and of my Drawing Number Two. Grown-ups never understand anything by themselves, and it is exhausting for children to have to provide explanations over and over again. So then I had to choose another career, and I learned to pilot airplanes. I have flown almost everywhere in the world. And, as a matter of fact, geography has been a big help to me. I could tell China from Arizona at first glance, which is very useful if you get lost during the night. So I have had in the course of my life, lots of encounters with lots of serious people. I have spent lots of time with grown-ups. I have seen them at close range... which hasn''t much improved my opinion of them."
Source: SAINT-EXUPÉRY, Antoine. The Little Prince. New York: Mariner Books, 1943.', 'About the career change portrayed in the text, it is possible to say that the narrator was', '[{"letra": "A", "texto": "aware of his piloting abilities."}, {"letra": "B", "texto": "encouraged to become a pilot."}, {"letra": "C", "texto": "more interested in piloting airplanes."}, {"letra": "D", "texto": "talked into learning a new profession."}]', 'D', 'O narrador relata que os adultos o aconselharam a desistir dos desenhos e se dedicar a disciplinas escolares, levando-o a abandonar a carreira artística e aprender a pilotar aviões. Ou seja, ele foi convencido/induzido a aprender uma nova profissão ("talked into learning a new profession").', NULL, NULL, 'resumida', FALSE, FALSE, 'ae2cf6908fce811e1ac05799217b009b96eed3e079ecf420e1d1c8e10004faa5', 'epcar-2025.txt'),
('EPCAR', 'Inglês', 'Reading Comprehension', 2025, NULL, 'EPCAR / FAB', 2, '02', NULL, 'facil', '(Utilizar o TEXTO I da Questão 1)', 'Grown-ups are not scared of the little prince''s drawings because', '[{"letra": "A", "texto": "snakes and hats don''t scare them."}, {"letra": "B", "texto": "they are always tougher than children."}, {"letra": "C", "texto": "they lack understanding of the pictures."}, {"letra": "D", "texto": "the second picture is scarier than the first one."}]', 'C', 'Os adultos não ficaram assustados porque interpretaram o desenho apenas como um chapéu, não compreendendo que se tratava de uma jiboia digerindo um elefante ("they lack understanding of the pictures").', NULL, NULL, 'resumida', FALSE, FALSE, 'bd016994a5cdbc6f7369b18b684290e35e44d5b4bfb284ce41ddb83cac2d3d4e', 'epcar-2025.txt'),
('EPCAR', 'Inglês', 'Funções da Linguagem', 2025, NULL, 'EPCAR / FAB', 3, '03', NULL, 'medio', '(Utilizar o TEXTO I da Questão 1)', 'The sentence that expresses disappointment is', '[{"letra": "A", "texto": "\"I have spent lots of time with grown-ups.\" (l. 22-23)"}, {"letra": "B", "texto": "\"Grown-ups never understand anything by themselves.\" (l. 14)"}, {"letra": "C", "texto": "\"And, as a matter of fact, geography has been a big help to me.\" (l. 18-19)"}, {"letra": "D", "texto": "\"Then I drew the inside of the boa constrictor, so the grown-ups could understand.\" (l. 5-6)"}]', 'B', 'A frase "Grown-ups never understand anything by themselves" expressa a decepção e frustração do narrador em relação à incapacidade dos adultos de compreenderem as coisas sozinhos sem explicações.', NULL, NULL, 'resumida', FALSE, FALSE, '6551e8eb9c3c3a9662d39fa39563736d0e39b2c0b367daa8cbcc0e7dd7019440', 'epcar-2025.txt'),
('EPCAR', 'Inglês', 'Reading Comprehension', 2025, NULL, 'EPCAR / FAB', 4, '04', NULL, 'facil', '(Utilizar o TEXTO I da Questão 1)', 'The narrator concludes that', '[{"letra": "A", "texto": "geography was his true passion."}, {"letra": "B", "texto": "he won''t change his mind about adults that easily."}, {"letra": "C", "texto": "adults end up understanding things by themselves."}, {"letra": "D", "texto": "his drawings weren''t that good due to his early age."}]', 'B', 'Na parte final do Texto I, o narrador afirma ter convivido muito com adultos de perto e que isso "não melhorou muito sua opinião sobre eles", concluindo que ele não mudará sua visão negativa sobre os adultos facilmente.', NULL, NULL, 'resumida', FALSE, FALSE, '3860b4b4d9989488da6d226a16868f2733f767f705c31e411096c343e579e771', 'epcar-2025.txt'),
('EPCAR', 'Inglês', 'Reading Comprehension', 2025, NULL, 'EPCAR / FAB', 5, '05', NULL, 'facil', 'TEXT II
Around the World with Barrier Breaking Women Pilots
Oct 31, 2021
By Dorothy Cochrane and P. Ramirez

We know the names of early American women pilots like Bessie Coleman and Amelia Earhart. However, across the globe, women pilots were also taking to the skies and setting records. Travel across with these stories of two such pilots.

Part I
Hélène Dutrieu, Belgium
Hélène Dutrieu of Belgium was known as the "girl hawk" of aviation because she was the most daring and accomplished woman pilot of her time. She first soloed in France in 1909 and within a year was setting altitude and distance records. She thrilled the world in September of 1910 by flying non-stop from Ostend to Bruges, Belgium, and she became the first Belgian woman to receive a pilot license on November 25, 1910. During her second year as an aviator she narrowly escaped death twice. She visited the United States in 1911, making her American debut at the Nassau Boulevard Aviation meeting. Back in Europe, Dutrieu won France''s Coupe Femina for the women''s world nonstop light record on December 31, 1911, flying 158 miles (254 kilometers) in 178 minutes. In Florence, Italy, she was the only woman in a field of 15 and outflew all of her male competitors to win the King''s Cup. In 1913 the French government awarded Dutrieu the Legion of Honor for her achievements. She also became an ambulance driver and later a director of a military hospital.

Part II
Jean Batten, New Zealand
Jean Batten grew up in New Zealand and developed a love for aviation that overcame her desire to be a concert pianist. Her interest in flying stemmed from the 1919 England to Australia flight by Ross and Keith Smith, and later solo flights. Batten''s father did not approve of her aviation enthusiasm but she convinced her mother to move to England with her and help her become a pilot. She received her license and her commercial rating at the London Aeroplane Club at Stag Lane and then began planning for a flight from England to Australia. Her first two attempts failed, but she succeeded in 1934, flying a Gipsy Moth. Batten became an instant sensation in Australia, New Zealand, and in England upon her return flight the next year (it was the first roundtrip by a woman). In 1935, she broke James Mollison''s records for England to Brazil and Dakar to Natal while becoming the first woman to solo across the South Atlantic. Then, in 1936, she realized her dream of flying solo from England to New Zealand in a Percival Gull in 11 days and earned her second consecutive Harmon Trophy, having shared the first one in 1935 with Amelia Earhart. In 1937, she set another record for an Australia to England flight. Unable to obtain a flying job during World War II, Batten gave up flying and eventually became a recluse, living with her mother in Majorca, Spain, and appearing in public only for a few anniversary events. In 1937, she published her autobiography, My Life.', 'Hélène Dutrieu received a nickname that was given to her due to', '[{"letra": "A", "texto": "her frequent solo flights."}, {"letra": "B", "texto": "some of her personal traits."}, {"letra": "C", "texto": "the fact she is a female pilot."}, {"letra": "D", "texto": "her friendship with Bessie Coleman."}]', 'B', 'O texto expressa explicitamente que Hélène Dutrieu foi chamada de "girl hawk" (garota falcão) por ser a mais ousada e realizada mulher piloto de sua época, ou seja, devido a traços de sua personalidade e bravura ("personal traits").', NULL, NULL, 'resumida', FALSE, FALSE, '45d15a032f0573b4e4882d9436fe23702618325962ce90b5c4faccc37d6f903f', 'epcar-2025.txt'),
('EPCAR', 'Inglês', 'Sequenciamento', 2025, NULL, 'EPCAR / FAB', 6, '06', NULL, 'medio', '(Utilizar o TEXTO II da Questão 5)', 'Regarding Jean Batten''s life, she
1. was discouraged by someone.
2. turned out to be a homebody.
